WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.610 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:02.440 align:middle line:84%
The following
content is provided

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:06.370 align:middle line:84%
by MIT OpenCourseWare under
a Creative Commons license.

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:08.470 align:middle line:84%
Additional information
about our license,

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:10.870 align:middle line:84%
and MIT OpenCourseWare
in general,

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:13.355 align:middle line:90%
is available at MIT.edu.

00:00:13.355 --> 00:00:21.230 align:middle line:90%


00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:22.940 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:26.220 align:middle line:90%
Welcome back to the second half.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:30.080 align:middle line:84%
We'll pick up on this
somewhat pessimistic note

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:36.860 align:middle line:84%
of how one goes from a very
accurate sequence to a probably

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:40.430 align:middle line:84%
less than accurate speculative
league and specificity,

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:43.910 align:middle line:84%
to actually how we actually
do get a 3D structure league

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:46.310 align:middle line:84%
and specificity, and some
of the powerful methods

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:52.220 align:middle line:84%
that are shared by the
computational tools.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:55.850 align:middle line:84%
One of the things we want to
do is we want to, if possible,

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:57.500 align:middle line:90%
find a homologue.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.050 align:middle line:84%
It may be a distant homologue
or a very close one.

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:02.660 align:middle line:84%
If it's very close, as we saw
from that previous slide about

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:08.210 align:middle line:84%
the highest accuracy coming from
homologues that are 80% or 90%.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:11.720 align:middle line:84%
As we get further and further
away, remember we had to--

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.820 align:middle line:84%
first, you could
use exact matches.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.760 align:middle line:84%
And then you had to use
dynamic programming, and then

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:18.200 align:middle line:90%
hidden Markov models.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.660 align:middle line:84%
And finally, at the
bottom of this Slide 37,

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.940 align:middle line:84%
we go to-- we
resort to threading,

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.850 align:middle line:84%
where one of the
structures is searched

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.370 align:middle line:84%
through a database of
three-dimensional structures

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:30.530 align:middle line:90%
with your favorite sequence.

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:34.100 align:middle line:84%
And you not only search through
the database of structures,

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.870 align:middle line:84%
but through each
structure, you think

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:38.120 align:middle line:84%
of every way you could
thread your sequence

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.310 align:middle line:84%
into that complicated
3D structure.

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:42.920 align:middle line:84%
And some of the
threading positions

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:46.010 align:middle line:84%
with insertions,
deletions, and offsets

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:47.280 align:middle line:90%
will be better than others.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:48.890 align:middle line:84%
They will fit that
three-dimensional structure.

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:51.620 align:middle line:84%
Some will cause clashes, where
the three-dimensional structure

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:54.440 align:middle line:84%
you're searching through
and the particular thread

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.840 align:middle line:84%
position you've got
causes two bulky groups

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.760 align:middle line:84%
to lie on top of
each other in space

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.543 align:middle line:84%
in a way that's hard
to relieve the stress.

00:02:01.543 --> 00:02:02.960 align:middle line:84%
And so, that
threading is probably

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.600 align:middle line:84%
the ultimate in getting
very distant relationships.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.670 align:middle line:84%
It is limited by the
fact that it really

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:09.282 align:middle line:84%
only works if one
of the two proteins

00:02:09.282 --> 00:02:10.699 align:middle line:84%
has a three-dimensional
structure.

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:13.700 align:middle line:84%
But you can search the database
of sequences against a database

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.200 align:middle line:90%
of 3D structures.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.110 align:middle line:90%


00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:22.160 align:middle line:84%
The antidote to the limitation
of having not enough

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:24.320 align:middle line:84%
three-dimensional
structures is to launch

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.540 align:middle line:84%
a project, like
the Genome Project,

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:30.200 align:middle line:84%
to get all of the
three-dimensional structures.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:35.520 align:middle line:90%
Now, is that finite?

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:37.590 align:middle line:84%
Well, certainly for
a particular genome,

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:41.610 align:middle line:84%
that number is less than or
equal to the number of proteins

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:45.280 align:middle line:84%
in that proteome for
that particular organism.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.070 align:middle line:84%
But if we look at
organisms as a whole,

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.830 align:middle line:84%
where we don't even really even
know how many organisms there

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:53.850 align:middle line:84%
are on Earth, then that
number may be larger.

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:56.550 align:middle line:84%
But some people estimate
that it's less than 10,000.

00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:00.420 align:middle line:84%
Basic folds, where a fold
is the scaffolding-- which,

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.430 align:middle line:84%
once you have that
fold, whether it's

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:04.680 align:middle line:84%
a certain number
of alpha helices,

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.570 align:middle line:84%
betas and turns in
a particular order,

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.040 align:middle line:90%
in a particular geometry.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.370 align:middle line:84%
Once you have that, and you
have any amino acid sequences

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:15.510 align:middle line:84%
within a 35% amino acid
identity-- and you'll see,

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:18.720 align:middle line:84%
as this last section
of this lecture

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.360 align:middle line:90%
goes along, why it's around 35%.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.090 align:middle line:90%


00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:29.220 align:middle line:84%
The goal is to get, saturate
the three-dimensional structure

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:31.410 align:middle line:90%
space, so that every three--

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.170 align:middle line:84%
every sequence is
within at least 35%

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:35.740 align:middle line:90%
of one of those structures.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:38.520 align:middle line:84%
This is somewhat conjectural,
unlike the Human Genome

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:43.230 align:middle line:84%
Project, where we knew we had
3 billion bases to sequence.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:46.890 align:middle line:84%
Here, we hope that we
have 10,000 basic folds.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:50.820 align:middle line:84%
And we hope that 35% amino
acid sequence identity will be

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.710 align:middle line:90%
enough to do homology modeling.

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:54.210 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:54.840 align:middle line:90%
It's not.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.760 align:middle line:90%
It's not currently.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:00.690 align:middle line:84%
And the criteria for this has
to be prioritized in some way.

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:03.960 align:middle line:84%
And remember, we had
prioritization for drug targets

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:05.680 align:middle line:90%
in the previous slide.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.850 align:middle line:84%
And here, prioritization for
structural genomics is similar.

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:11.625 align:middle line:84%
But in addition,
you want to have--

00:04:11.625 --> 00:04:14.100 align:middle line:84%
you want them to
represent the largest

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.250 align:middle line:84%
family you can get, but not
have it previously solved.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:22.830 align:middle line:90%


00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:26.400 align:middle line:84%
And for some reason,
they're excluding non--

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.510 align:middle line:84%
or they're excluding
transmembrane proteins.

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:30.720 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is a very
important class,

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:34.200 align:middle line:90%
as we'll see in the next slide.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:38.550 align:middle line:84%
Because the goal stated
up at the top of this,

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:42.180 align:middle line:84%
Slide 38, of assigning
functions and interpreting

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:45.390 align:middle line:84%
disease related polymorphisms
and drug targets

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:50.440 align:middle line:84%
and so on certainly apply to
membrane proteins as well.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:53.160 align:middle line:84%
And there are reasons that
we've already alluded to

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.320 align:middle line:84%
for looking specific
for programming

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:56.710 align:middle line:90%
cells via membrane proteins.

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:58.845 align:middle line:84%
This is where cell-cell
interactions occur.

00:04:58.845 --> 00:05:02.760 align:middle line:84%
This is where adhesion motility
immune recognition occurs.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.700 align:middle line:84%
These all occur without
getting inside of the cell.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:11.160 align:middle line:90%


00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:15.200 align:middle line:84%
This is a major class
of drug targets.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.670 align:middle line:84%
And furthermore, it's not like
this is an impossible class

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:20.930 align:middle line:90%
of proteins to solve.

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:24.530 align:middle line:84%
Actually, the three-dimensional
structural databases--

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:26.120 align:middle line:90%
more about that in a moment--

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:27.690 align:middle line:90%
are filled with these things.

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:31.040 align:middle line:84%
They're certainly-- are the
most underrepresented class,

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:32.630 align:middle line:84%
but there are
plenty of examples.

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:34.920 align:middle line:90%
And there are two major classes.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:38.510 align:middle line:84%
One of them is soluble fragments
of fibrous or membrane protein.

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:41.180 align:middle line:84%
Some of the fibrous proteins
are excluded as well.

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:43.820 align:middle line:84%
Here you'll use a protease
to cleave off, possibly,

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:47.030 align:middle line:84%
a tiny piece that
makes it insoluble,

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:48.710 align:middle line:84%
maybe a little anchor
into the membrane.

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:51.127 align:middle line:84%
And all the rest of it now
behaves like a soluble protein.

00:05:51.127 --> 00:05:54.260 align:middle line:84%
And we know how to
solve soluble proteins.

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:57.560 align:middle line:84%
Other times, the other class
is integral membrane proteins,

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:03.500 align:middle line:84%
where they go like
this one bacteria

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:05.370 align:middle line:84%
on the right-hand
side of the image,

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:07.010 align:middle line:84%
you can see the
red alpha helices

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:11.240 align:middle line:84%
go back and forth across the
membrane where the gray lipids.

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:14.060 align:middle line:84%
And this is-- there's no
way you could clip off

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:16.310 align:middle line:84%
a little piece of this and
have a major fraction of it

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:16.940 align:middle line:90%
left to solve.

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:18.722 align:middle line:84%
But this was solved
in the membrane.

00:06:18.722 --> 00:06:20.930 align:middle line:84%
And you can see the little
blue water molecules going

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:24.800 align:middle line:90%
through that channel in this.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:27.320 align:middle line:84%
That channel is
responsible for a proton

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:31.213 align:middle line:84%
pumping, which can be part of
the ATP production process.

00:06:31.213 --> 00:06:32.630 align:middle line:84%
But there are many
other classes--

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:39.170 align:middle line:84%
redox proteins, toxins, ion
channels, photosynthesis

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:43.340 align:middle line:84%
and phototransduction,
and so on.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:46.280 align:middle line:84%
The G protein
coupled-receptor class

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:48.080 align:middle line:84%
is a particularly
important drug target.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:53.780 align:middle line:84%
ABC proteins and transporters
have also been solved.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:57.860 align:middle line:84%
So given that this is an
underrepresented class,

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:05.090 align:middle line:84%
and given that the structural
genomics project will not

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:09.680 align:middle line:84%
necessarily target
these in a rapid manner,

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:11.180 align:middle line:84%
what is the current
state of affairs

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:16.430 align:middle line:84%
for computational prediction
of the transmembrane regions

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:18.080 align:middle line:90%
of proteins?

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:21.050 align:middle line:84%
And actually, I would
say the prospects here

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:22.910 align:middle line:84%
are fairly favorable
compared to some

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:25.940 align:middle line:84%
of the least favorable ones
in that slide, that very

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:27.740 align:middle line:90%
pessimistic slide a few back.

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:33.110 align:middle line:84%
Here, you can get, as
indicated in this JMP paper,

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:35.720 align:middle line:84%
transmembrane
helices identified--

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:38.570 align:middle line:84%
and there can also be
transmembrane beta, as well--

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:42.230 align:middle line:84%
but helices identify with
accuracy greater than 99%.

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:46.100 align:middle line:84%
And remember, this is basically
saying that you correctly

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:47.280 align:middle line:90%
identified those.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:49.250 align:middle line:84%
And then you also have
false predictions.

00:07:49.250 --> 00:07:55.070 align:middle line:84%
There'll be a number
of peptide regions

00:07:55.070 --> 00:07:57.680 align:middle line:84%
of known proteins, which
are incorrectly predicted

00:07:57.680 --> 00:07:58.790 align:middle line:90%
to be in transmembrane.

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:03.710 align:middle line:84%
And this is a tolerable false
prediction rate of 17% to 43%,

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:08.150 align:middle line:84%
given a set of soluble proteins
as a negative learning set.

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:14.730 align:middle line:90%


00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:17.670 align:middle line:84%
Now, that's-- just merely
knowing that a particular

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:20.400 align:middle line:84%
segment of protein is
transmembrane is a big step

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:22.330 align:middle line:84%
in terms of identifying
its function.

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:26.790 align:middle line:84%
But to get the further
functional characterization,

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:28.330 align:middle line:84%
we need things like
ligand binding,

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:29.760 align:middle line:90%
which we've already addressed.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:31.950 align:middle line:84%
And if you look at
some of these quotes,

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:33.929 align:middle line:84%
you can see that a
lot of the emphasis

00:08:33.929 --> 00:08:42.030 align:middle line:84%
is on display and cataloging
and a hopeful expectation

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:47.670 align:middle line:84%
that we'll be able to move
from rough three-dimensional

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:52.470 align:middle line:84%
structures to
ligand-binding specificity.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:54.630 align:middle line:84%
But where do the
three-dimensional structures

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:57.030 align:middle line:84%
that we do have, do
believe, that do tell us

00:08:57.030 --> 00:09:00.130 align:middle line:84%
about the exact geometry of
ligand binding come from?

00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:01.500 align:middle line:90%
Where do they come from?

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:03.420 align:middle line:90%
And how do we compute on them?

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:04.525 align:middle line:90%
How do we read them?

00:09:04.525 --> 00:09:06.150 align:middle line:84%
How do our computer
programs read them?

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:11.820 align:middle line:84%
Well, this is a typical file of
a three-dimensional structure.

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:13.417 align:middle line:84%
This happens to be
one that we will

00:09:13.417 --> 00:09:15.330 align:middle line:84%
show at the-- the
three-dimensional structure

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:17.070 align:middle line:90%
at the very end of this talk.

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:22.270 align:middle line:84%
It is the human
estrogen receptor.

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:24.600 align:middle line:84%
And you can see,
the first line is

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:29.340 align:middle line:84%
that it is a complex between
a protein and a DNA molecule.

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:32.760 align:middle line:84%
And the molecules,
the estrogen receptor.

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:35.620 align:middle line:84%
The third line down
is the resolution.

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:39.420 align:middle line:84%
This is a technical description
of the X-ray diffraction

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:40.920 align:middle line:90%
pattern.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:44.880 align:middle line:84%
It gives you an upper
limit to how precise it is.

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:47.460 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be more
precise than 2.4 angstroms,

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:49.650 align:middle line:84%
depending on how much
statistical oversampling

00:09:49.650 --> 00:09:53.220 align:middle line:84%
you have, and how good your
computer program is enforcing

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:54.900 align:middle line:90%
their chemical constraints.

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:58.650 align:middle line:84%
A typical precision for a 2.4
angstrom protein structure

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:01.890 align:middle line:84%
might be on the order
of 0.3 angstroms,

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:05.070 align:middle line:84%
maybe eight times better
than the nominal resolution.

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:09.180 align:middle line:84%
But that's an important number,
unambiguously determined

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:10.513 align:middle line:90%
in the process of collecting it.

00:10:10.513 --> 00:10:11.972 align:middle line:84%
So when you look
at the literature,

00:10:11.972 --> 00:10:13.050 align:middle line:90%
look at this number.

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:14.620 align:middle line:84%
And look at the
next number down,

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:18.150 align:middle line:84%
which is the R value, which is
not a measure of resolution,

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:22.150 align:middle line:84%
but a measure of goodness
of fit between the model.

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:25.170 align:middle line:84%
The model is the X, Y,
Z-coordinates of your atoms.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:28.840 align:middle line:84%
It's the goodness of fit
between the model and the data.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:30.840 align:middle line:84%
And we'll have a
slide coming up soon

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:34.470 align:middle line:84%
of how this R value
is calculated.

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:35.100 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:37.740 align:middle line:84%
The next line down
begins the sequence.

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:41.520 align:middle line:84%
And if you have a multi-chain
sequence, here I've cut--

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:43.290 align:middle line:90%
I've cut out some lines for--

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:45.630 align:middle line:84%
there's many lines of
sequence for the protein

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:47.520 align:middle line:84%
in this three-letter
code, and main lines

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:50.310 align:middle line:84%
of sequence for the nucleic
acid in this one-letter code.

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:54.090 align:middle line:84%
And then, additional chemical
parts of the structure.

00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:55.960 align:middle line:84%
Remember, the structure
is complicated.

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:57.360 align:middle line:90%
It's not just protein.

00:10:57.360 --> 00:10:59.850 align:middle line:84%
Here it has nucleic
acid, has zinc.

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:03.150 align:middle line:84%
It has water molecules,
sometimes various other things.

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:07.260 align:middle line:84%
Each of these molecules, if you
can find it in the structure,

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:09.840 align:middle line:84%
you will determine the
X, Y and Z-coordinates.

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:12.360 align:middle line:84%
So the next one tells you
the secondary structure.

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:15.330 align:middle line:84%
Remember, that there's three
basic types-- alpha helices,

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:17.370 align:middle line:90%
beta sheets, and coils.

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:20.460 align:middle line:84%
And these are described-- and
again, I'm just showing you

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:22.180 align:middle line:90%
one line example of each.

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:25.110 align:middle line:84%
There's a long list for
each of these, where they've

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:27.390 align:middle line:84%
been identified
by either manually

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:30.930 align:middle line:84%
or a computational automation
from the structure.

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:37.320 align:middle line:84%
And these can be useful as
a summary of the structure.

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:39.960 align:middle line:84%
And then, here's the real
meat of the structure.

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:43.140 align:middle line:84%
The lines that begin
with the word atom, this

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:45.900 align:middle line:84%
is a position of the
nitrogen atom number

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:49.110 align:middle line:84%
one in methionine, which
is the amino acid number

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:51.570 align:middle line:90%
one in the A chain.

00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:57.990 align:middle line:84%
So it met A. The A-chain
happens to be the protein chain.

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:03.221 align:middle line:84%
And then, following that
is the residue number one.

00:12:03.221 --> 00:12:03.721 align:middle line:90%


00:12:03.721 --> 00:12:08.760 align:middle line:84%
And then, XYZ coordinates,
roughly 50, 24, 79.

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:11.880 align:middle line:84%
Then a scale factor one,
which is almost always one.

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:14.700 align:middle line:84%
And then a B
factor, 60, which is

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:20.220 align:middle line:84%
representative of how far from
that XYZ value can it deviate?

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:24.330 align:middle line:90%
That's a square deviation term.

00:12:24.330 --> 00:12:29.610 align:middle line:84%
And it absorbs
the thermal motion

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:32.620 align:middle line:84%
of that atom and various
structural defects.

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:35.970 align:middle line:84%
So it gives you some idea of
the disorder of that atom.

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:39.540 align:middle line:84%
And then you have the last
couple of records have to do,

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:44.760 align:middle line:84%
what atom is connected to
what atom in the structure?

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:46.260 align:middle line:84%
In a certain sense,
those can often

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:50.570 align:middle line:84%
be inferred just by the distance
between atoms in the structure.

00:12:50.570 --> 00:12:52.650 align:middle line:84%
Now on the far right
hand side, it's

00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:58.620 align:middle line:84%
just the record number and the
shorthand for the structure,

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:00.330 align:middle line:90%
which is 1/8 CQ.

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:04.620 align:middle line:84%
1/8 CQ refers to the
human chicken receptor.

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:07.800 align:middle line:90%


00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:09.840 align:middle line:90%
So that's a very dry--

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:14.160 align:middle line:84%
that's the way that it
appears when you download it

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:19.950 align:middle line:90%
from the database PDB or RCSV.

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:23.790 align:middle line:84%
Then when you display it,
while you're solving it,

00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:26.430 align:middle line:84%
if you're NMR or
X-ray crystallographer

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:29.970 align:middle line:84%
or possibly display
it from the databases,

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:33.720 align:middle line:84%
and the two different cultures
in [INAUDIBLE] on the left

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:37.800 align:middle line:84%
tends to describe their
structures as multiple chain

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:41.340 align:middle line:84%
tracings because they
want to either express

00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:43.800 align:middle line:84%
their uncertainty
of the structure,

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:46.560 align:middle line:84%
or they want to brag
about how they know

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:48.030 align:middle line:90%
something about the dynamics.

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:50.610 align:middle line:84%
Whatever, you have
multiple chains

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:53.640 align:middle line:84%
which overlap here in
different colors indicating

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:55.350 align:middle line:84%
some of the
different uncertainty

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:59.130 align:middle line:90%
or dynamics of each major atom.

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:01.680 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes you'll show all the
atoms as one on the right,

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:03.330 align:middle line:84%
or you'll just show
the major atoms

00:14:03.330 --> 00:14:06.660 align:middle line:84%
like the carbon alphas which is
the center of each amino acid

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:08.880 align:middle line:90%
as you go along on the left.

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:12.030 align:middle line:84%
On the right is the way
that X-ray crystallographer

00:14:12.030 --> 00:14:15.910 align:middle line:90%
might show the fit to the data.

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:19.680 align:middle line:84%
So the model is a stick
figure connecting the atoms

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:21.660 align:middle line:90%
as circles.

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:24.580 align:middle line:84%
And then the mesh work
is the electron density,

00:14:24.580 --> 00:14:29.010 align:middle line:84%
which you can observe once
you have all the X-ray

00:14:29.010 --> 00:14:33.940 align:middle line:84%
data and the model, or
you can calculate it

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:35.010 align:middle line:90%
once you have the model.

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:39.372 align:middle line:84%
The model plus the known physics
of scattering of each of the--

00:14:39.372 --> 00:14:41.830 align:middle line:84%
known physics of the electron
density of each of the atoms,

00:14:41.830 --> 00:14:43.980 align:middle line:84%
you can calculate
electron density.

00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:47.610 align:middle line:84%
Now you can compare the
calculated electron density

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:48.480 align:middle line:90%
with the observed.

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:51.390 align:middle line:84%
Or you can compare the
calculated scattering

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:53.520 align:middle line:90%
with the observed.

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:55.570 align:middle line:84%
Typically, it's done
in the scattering,

00:14:55.570 --> 00:14:58.440 align:middle line:84%
which is the 4E transform
of the electron density.

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:00.630 align:middle line:90%
And that's all this is.

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:03.090 align:middle line:84%
The electron density is
indicated by RHO here

00:15:03.090 --> 00:15:04.770 align:middle line:90%
in the middle of this formula.

00:15:04.770 --> 00:15:07.800 align:middle line:84%
And the 4E transform
is just this integral

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:12.000 align:middle line:84%
of RHO over the
phasing information,

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:16.680 align:middle line:90%
the phasing of the light waves.

00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:20.010 align:middle line:84%
These waves, just like a
wave on an ocean has a phase.

00:15:20.010 --> 00:15:22.870 align:middle line:84%
Whether it's up or down
in the trough, how much.

00:15:22.870 --> 00:15:27.040 align:middle line:84%
And so the product of
the RHO electron density,

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:30.180 align:middle line:84%
which is a function of X, Y,
and Z, all three coordinates,

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:33.060 align:middle line:84%
its is summed by
these integrals--

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:37.310 align:middle line:84%
It's a continuous
function-- from 0 to 1.

00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:40.268 align:middle line:84%
0 to 1, and X, Y,
and Z. This is Y01,

00:15:40.268 --> 00:15:42.060 align:middle line:84%
it's because this is
a repeating structure.

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:48.660 align:middle line:84%
It has a little rectangular
cube of space around it,

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:49.595 align:middle line:90%
which repeats.

00:15:49.595 --> 00:15:51.470 align:middle line:84%
And so all you really
need to do to calculate

00:15:51.470 --> 00:15:53.480 align:middle line:84%
the entire electron
density is to think

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:55.720 align:middle line:84%
about this little
cube, which goes from 0

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:57.140 align:middle line:90%
to 1 in those arbitrary units.

00:15:57.140 --> 00:15:59.690 align:middle line:90%


00:15:59.690 --> 00:16:02.930 align:middle line:84%
But now that's how you can
get from one space to another.

00:16:02.930 --> 00:16:05.030 align:middle line:84%
From the electron
density to the scattering

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:07.160 align:middle line:84%
that you actually
observe when you

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:11.760 align:middle line:84%
shine x-ray light upon a
repeating crystal structure.

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:13.460 align:middle line:84%
But now you want to
adjust the model.

00:16:13.460 --> 00:16:16.740 align:middle line:84%
Adjust those atoms
in the previous slide

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:20.400 align:middle line:84%
so that you can
maximize the fit.

00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:22.170 align:middle line:84%
That is to say,
minimize the difference

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:24.930 align:middle line:84%
between the observed
scattering F0

00:16:24.930 --> 00:16:26.753 align:middle line:84%
and the calculated
scattering, FC.

00:16:26.753 --> 00:16:28.920 align:middle line:84%
Because you know the
scattering of each atom and you

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:30.750 align:middle line:84%
know that you're
trying to determine

00:16:30.750 --> 00:16:32.070 align:middle line:90%
the position of each atom.

00:16:32.070 --> 00:16:36.870 align:middle line:84%
The position of the atom
might be a parameter, P,

00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:39.390 align:middle line:84%
which you adjust a
small bit at a time.

00:16:39.390 --> 00:16:41.990 align:middle line:90%


00:16:41.990 --> 00:16:46.730 align:middle line:84%
And that and that change
can be approximated

00:16:46.730 --> 00:16:50.060 align:middle line:84%
by what's called a
Paler Series Expansion.

00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:52.010 align:middle line:84%
Here, we're just taking
the first term, which

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:54.590 align:middle line:84%
involves first derivatives,
all sub-sequence terms

00:16:54.590 --> 00:16:56.270 align:middle line:84%
involve second
derivatives and higher.

00:16:56.270 --> 00:17:00.470 align:middle line:84%
And those are close
enough to 0 for this work

00:17:00.470 --> 00:17:02.750 align:middle line:90%
that you drop them.

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:04.730 align:middle line:84%
And this basically
says that if we're

00:17:04.730 --> 00:17:06.890 align:middle line:84%
going to adjust
this parameter, we

00:17:06.890 --> 00:17:10.560 align:middle line:84%
can get a feeling for
how fast to adjust it,

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:13.910 align:middle line:84%
which is based on the
sensitivity of the scattering,

00:17:13.910 --> 00:17:16.940 align:middle line:84%
the F. The derivative
of F calculated

00:17:16.940 --> 00:17:18.859 align:middle line:90%
with respect to each parameter.

00:17:18.859 --> 00:17:21.230 align:middle line:84%
The parameters would be
X, Y, and Z-coordinates,

00:17:21.230 --> 00:17:25.972 align:middle line:84%
or they could be some kind
of rotational parameters.

00:17:25.972 --> 00:17:28.430 align:middle line:84%
So this is to give you a flavor
for how it's actually done.

00:17:28.430 --> 00:17:32.030 align:middle line:84%
This is how you actually
get from the scattering off

00:17:32.030 --> 00:17:32.630 align:middle line:90%
of a crystal.

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:33.980 align:middle line:90%
The crystal has the advantage.

00:17:33.980 --> 00:17:35.980 align:middle line:84%
In principle, you can do
a scattering experiment

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:37.490 align:middle line:90%
from single molecules.

00:17:37.490 --> 00:17:40.280 align:middle line:84%
But single molecules,
the signal is too weak.

00:17:40.280 --> 00:17:44.630 align:middle line:84%
And it's swamped out by the
noise of random other photon

00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:46.040 align:middle line:90%
events.

00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:49.460 align:middle line:84%
So by having a large number
of them in ordered array,

00:17:49.460 --> 00:17:52.880 align:middle line:84%
they all cohere
and they basically

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:54.350 align:middle line:90%
do your statistics for you.

00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:59.510 align:middle line:84%
They integrate and
you get the value

00:17:59.510 --> 00:18:02.032 align:middle line:84%
of the statistics of
billions of molecules

00:18:02.032 --> 00:18:04.490 align:middle line:84%
without having to observe each
of the billions of molecules

00:18:04.490 --> 00:18:07.550 align:middle line:84%
and then do the computation in
the presence of a huge noise.

00:18:07.550 --> 00:18:09.800 align:middle line:84%
So that's what the
crystal is all about.

00:18:09.800 --> 00:18:12.150 align:middle line:84%
NMR also requires
billions of molecules.

00:18:12.150 --> 00:18:13.760 align:middle line:84%
And so they both
have the big demand

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:18.470 align:middle line:84%
of requiring large
amounts of pure molecules.

00:18:18.470 --> 00:18:20.660 align:middle line:84%
And that's one of the reasons
that membrane proteins

00:18:20.660 --> 00:18:22.550 align:middle line:90%
have been harder to get at.

00:18:22.550 --> 00:18:26.810 align:middle line:84%
It's harder to get large
amounts of pure membrane pieces.

00:18:26.810 --> 00:18:30.650 align:middle line:84%
Now these two
methods NMR, which I

00:18:30.650 --> 00:18:33.380 align:middle line:84%
won't describe, and extra
crystallography that I barely

00:18:33.380 --> 00:18:36.530 align:middle line:84%
described, share with
the abinitio methods

00:18:36.530 --> 00:18:42.650 align:middle line:84%
of protein structure prediction
certain key computational

00:18:42.650 --> 00:18:44.790 align:middle line:90%
components.

00:18:44.790 --> 00:18:47.930 align:middle line:84%
And these are embedded in
a combined system, which

00:18:47.930 --> 00:18:50.060 align:middle line:84%
does crystallography,
NMR, and some

00:18:50.060 --> 00:18:52.340 align:middle line:84%
of these molecular mechanics
that keeps the structure.

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:56.930 align:middle line:84%
You can imagine that if the
structure started blowing off,

00:18:56.930 --> 00:18:58.550 align:middle line:84%
atoms going in
weird directions, it

00:18:58.550 --> 00:19:00.660 align:middle line:84%
could still minimize
the function

00:19:00.660 --> 00:19:02.040 align:middle line:90%
if you have a local minimum.

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:04.910 align:middle line:84%
But if you hold the
chemistry intact

00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:07.340 align:middle line:84%
and satisfy what you know
about molecule mechanics

00:19:07.340 --> 00:19:11.030 align:middle line:84%
of chemicals, then you
actually can fit a structure

00:19:11.030 --> 00:19:13.700 align:middle line:90%
from further away.

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:16.280 align:middle line:84%
Now here's the ARC factor I
said we will come back to.

00:19:16.280 --> 00:19:17.960 align:middle line:84%
As you do this
refinement, as you

00:19:17.960 --> 00:19:20.090 align:middle line:84%
adjust the positions
of each of the atoms,

00:19:20.090 --> 00:19:22.190 align:middle line:84%
as your computer adjusts
the position of each

00:19:22.190 --> 00:19:25.910 align:middle line:84%
of these atoms, you
compare the scattering

00:19:25.910 --> 00:19:30.680 align:middle line:90%
of the observed F0 with the FC.

00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:32.750 align:middle line:84%
These are in absolute
values because actually,

00:19:32.750 --> 00:19:36.110 align:middle line:84%
the scattering results in
loss of phase information.

00:19:36.110 --> 00:19:39.140 align:middle line:84%
So the actual things you
measure are absolute values.

00:19:39.140 --> 00:19:41.390 align:middle line:84%
And then you take the absolute
value of the difference

00:19:41.390 --> 00:19:45.980 align:middle line:84%
in order to make sure you
sum a positive number.

00:19:45.980 --> 00:19:50.360 align:middle line:84%
And then you normalize
this, as we did before,

00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:55.375 align:middle line:84%
to put it on a recognizable
standard scale where 0.4 means

00:19:55.375 --> 00:19:56.750 align:middle line:84%
you have a very
crude structure--

00:19:56.750 --> 00:19:58.370 align:middle line:84%
if you see this in the
literature you don't believe

00:19:58.370 --> 00:19:58.870 align:middle line:90%
it.

00:19:58.870 --> 00:20:02.510 align:middle line:84%
If it's less than 0.25,
which the last structure was,

00:20:02.510 --> 00:20:06.620 align:middle line:84%
then you believe it as
pretty close to done.

00:20:06.620 --> 00:20:08.808 align:middle line:84%
This is very analogous to
correlation coefficient.

00:20:08.808 --> 00:20:10.850 align:middle line:84%
Remember, we had a linear
correlation coefficient

00:20:10.850 --> 00:20:12.110 align:middle line:84%
between two
functions, here would

00:20:12.110 --> 00:20:13.280 align:middle line:90%
be observed and calculated.

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:16.730 align:middle line:84%
If they correlate well, then
you're getting close to done.

00:20:16.730 --> 00:20:20.180 align:middle line:84%
Correlating well is better
than 0.7, in this case.

00:20:20.180 --> 00:20:23.713 align:middle line:84%
And one way of reporting
the similarities between two

00:20:23.713 --> 00:20:25.130 align:middle line:84%
structures, this
is not a goodness

00:20:25.130 --> 00:20:26.588 align:middle line:84%
of fit between
model and data, this

00:20:26.588 --> 00:20:29.390 align:middle line:84%
is a goodness of fit
between two models.

00:20:29.390 --> 00:20:32.300 align:middle line:84%
Atom by atom you go
through and you measure

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:34.910 align:middle line:90%
the distance between them.

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:38.290 align:middle line:84%
And that root mean
squared deviation,

00:20:38.290 --> 00:20:40.300 align:middle line:84%
of all the distances
over all the atoms,

00:20:40.300 --> 00:20:44.110 align:middle line:84%
or all the key atoms, core
atoms, carbon alphas--

00:20:44.110 --> 00:20:46.870 align:middle line:84%
or maybe even a smaller
core than that--

00:20:46.870 --> 00:20:51.940 align:middle line:84%
allows you to quote a root
mean square deviation, which

00:20:51.940 --> 00:20:56.470 align:middle line:84%
has some meaning independent
of how many atoms you have

00:20:56.470 --> 00:20:58.210 align:middle line:84%
and what proteins
you're looking at.

00:20:58.210 --> 00:21:00.460 align:middle line:84%
Each of these is try to
put this on a common scale

00:21:00.460 --> 00:21:04.905 align:middle line:84%
so you can compare from
structure to structure.

00:21:04.905 --> 00:21:07.030 align:middle line:84%
Now if we're going to do
molecular mechanics, which

00:21:07.030 --> 00:21:12.670 align:middle line:84%
is common to the computational
empirical methods

00:21:12.670 --> 00:21:15.490 align:middle line:84%
and the computational
sequence based methods,

00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:23.620 align:middle line:84%
we need to talk about the
side chains of the proteins.

00:21:23.620 --> 00:21:26.590 align:middle line:84%
We've mainly been talking
about the backbones.

00:21:26.590 --> 00:21:28.990 align:middle line:84%
And just a refresher, this
is from the geniculate code.

00:21:28.990 --> 00:21:31.150 align:middle line:84%
Again, the blue or
the positively charge

00:21:31.150 --> 00:21:32.860 align:middle line:84%
and the negative
charge and so forth.

00:21:32.860 --> 00:21:35.290 align:middle line:90%
They have a chirality.

00:21:35.290 --> 00:21:38.170 align:middle line:84%
It matters whether you're
talking about L-amino acids

00:21:38.170 --> 00:21:39.250 align:middle line:90%
or D-amino acids.

00:21:39.250 --> 00:21:40.840 align:middle line:84%
The way you remember--
this is just

00:21:40.840 --> 00:21:42.670 align:middle line:84%
a mnemonic for
remembering it-- is

00:21:42.670 --> 00:21:45.250 align:middle line:84%
that when the hydrogens point
towards you, going clockwise,

00:21:45.250 --> 00:21:51.340 align:middle line:84%
it goes C0 carbonyl R, this
is a side chain in corn.

00:21:51.340 --> 00:21:56.140 align:middle line:84%
And some of the 19
of these amino acids

00:21:56.140 --> 00:22:00.640 align:middle line:90%
have a chirality there.

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:04.870 align:middle line:84%
Glycine does not because it has
two hydrogens instead of an R,

00:22:04.870 --> 00:22:08.140 align:middle line:84%
it has a hydrogen. And
two of the amino acids

00:22:08.140 --> 00:22:11.020 align:middle line:84%
actually have two centers
of chiral asymmetry.

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:19.150 align:middle line:84%
Threonine, which has this
side chain, and Isoleucine.

00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:21.310 align:middle line:84%
They have the carbon
alpha and the carbon beta

00:22:21.310 --> 00:22:23.290 align:middle line:90%
are both asymmetric.

00:22:23.290 --> 00:22:25.480 align:middle line:84%
And one of the very
earliest exercises

00:22:25.480 --> 00:22:28.720 align:middle line:84%
was done when the very
first models of proteins

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:30.540 align:middle line:90%
were looked at, little peptides.

00:22:30.540 --> 00:22:33.880 align:middle line:90%


00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:37.690 align:middle line:84%
Can do this by hand, with
some very simple crude models.

00:22:37.690 --> 00:22:40.210 align:middle line:84%
And you can go through
systematically,

00:22:40.210 --> 00:22:45.820 align:middle line:84%
there are three bonds along
a peptide, long peptide.

00:22:45.820 --> 00:22:48.230 align:middle line:84%
And these are the
peptide bond itself,

00:22:48.230 --> 00:22:50.980 align:middle line:84%
which connects one amino
acid to the next one.

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:52.440 align:middle line:84%
And that tends to
be pretty rigid.

00:22:52.440 --> 00:22:54.410 align:middle line:84%
It has a partial
double stranded bond

00:22:54.410 --> 00:22:57.010 align:middle line:84%
and it tends to be a
trans-configuration, 180

00:22:57.010 --> 00:22:57.920 align:middle line:90%
degrees.

00:22:57.920 --> 00:23:00.770 align:middle line:84%
This is the rotation around
the bonds, not the bond angle,

00:23:00.770 --> 00:23:02.470 align:middle line:84%
but the rotation
around the bonds.

00:23:02.470 --> 00:23:07.000 align:middle line:84%
And there are two other
bonds that are not so rigid.

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:09.490 align:middle line:84%
So these are free, but
they're constrained

00:23:09.490 --> 00:23:12.250 align:middle line:84%
by the clashes that occur
that when you rotate around

00:23:12.250 --> 00:23:16.960 align:middle line:84%
the bond, the side
chains will clash

00:23:16.960 --> 00:23:19.220 align:middle line:90%
with other parts of the protein.

00:23:19.220 --> 00:23:21.850 align:middle line:84%
And so Ramachandran and
colleagues went through

00:23:21.850 --> 00:23:25.100 align:middle line:84%
systematically, all the
possible Phi and PSI angles,

00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:26.950 align:middle line:90%
these are these two free bonds.

00:23:26.950 --> 00:23:30.850 align:middle line:84%
And this is shown here ranging
over the full range of Phi

00:23:30.850 --> 00:23:33.850 align:middle line:84%
and PSI on the
horizontal vertical axis.

00:23:33.850 --> 00:23:35.620 align:middle line:84%
And you get these
little orange regions

00:23:35.620 --> 00:23:40.120 align:middle line:84%
where even with very bulky
chain groups, which would occur

00:23:40.120 --> 00:23:42.190 align:middle line:90%
you get these allowed regions.

00:23:42.190 --> 00:23:44.290 align:middle line:84%
And these two allowed
regions happened

00:23:44.290 --> 00:23:47.500 align:middle line:84%
to coincide with two of
the most popular motifs you

00:23:47.500 --> 00:23:50.920 align:middle line:84%
find in proteins, which are the
beta sheet and the alpha helix.

00:23:50.920 --> 00:23:55.480 align:middle line:90%


00:23:55.480 --> 00:23:57.610 align:middle line:84%
There are other things,
such as the 310 helix

00:23:57.610 --> 00:23:59.500 align:middle line:84%
and various other
structures that turn up.

00:23:59.500 --> 00:24:01.720 align:middle line:84%
But those are by far
the two most common.

00:24:01.720 --> 00:24:03.130 align:middle line:84%
And the yellow
shows how they get

00:24:03.130 --> 00:24:05.088 align:middle line:84%
extended when you have
smaller side chains that

00:24:05.088 --> 00:24:09.580 align:middle line:84%
allow more parts of
the conformation spaces

00:24:09.580 --> 00:24:12.430 align:middle line:90%
as it's called, to be inspected.

00:24:12.430 --> 00:24:14.320 align:middle line:84%
Now that's a very crude
thing that you can do

00:24:14.320 --> 00:24:16.105 align:middle line:90%
with very simple stick figures.

00:24:16.105 --> 00:24:20.340 align:middle line:90%


00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:24.360 align:middle line:84%
But as you get to more
detailed analysis,

00:24:24.360 --> 00:24:28.170 align:middle line:84%
the ultimate application of
all we know about physics,

00:24:28.170 --> 00:24:32.040 align:middle line:84%
if we could compute them would
be quantum electrodynamics.

00:24:32.040 --> 00:24:35.730 align:middle line:84%
This is way out of range
for any molecule of the size

00:24:35.730 --> 00:24:37.150 align:middle line:90%
that we're interested in.

00:24:37.150 --> 00:24:38.730 align:middle line:84%
And then as you
go down this list,

00:24:38.730 --> 00:24:40.590 align:middle line:84%
you get more and
more precise programs

00:24:40.590 --> 00:24:42.600 align:middle line:84%
until you get down
to something which

00:24:42.600 --> 00:24:45.720 align:middle line:84%
is barely computationally
feasible for things

00:24:45.720 --> 00:24:46.710 align:middle line:90%
the size of proteins.

00:24:46.710 --> 00:24:52.050 align:middle line:84%
And a great approximation
of all the quantum

00:24:52.050 --> 00:24:53.275 align:middle line:90%
approximations above it.

00:24:53.275 --> 00:24:54.900 align:middle line:84%
Every one of these
is an approximation,

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:58.115 align:middle line:84%
but each one as you go down,
gets more and more approximate.

00:24:58.115 --> 00:24:59.490 align:middle line:84%
And the main thing
that's missing

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:02.130 align:middle line:84%
from molecular mechanics
that's present in the next step

00:25:02.130 --> 00:25:04.920 align:middle line:84%
up is the polarization
of electrons.

00:25:04.920 --> 00:25:07.770 align:middle line:84%
In other words, in
micro mechanics,

00:25:07.770 --> 00:25:10.740 align:middle line:84%
you assume the electron clouds
are basically spherical.

00:25:10.740 --> 00:25:16.830 align:middle line:84%
And this is a huge loss, but it
still is computationally very

00:25:16.830 --> 00:25:17.580 align:middle line:90%
demanding.

00:25:17.580 --> 00:25:20.310 align:middle line:84%
So you don't get that
asymmetric polarization

00:25:20.310 --> 00:25:27.880 align:middle line:84%
that you get in hydrogen
bonds and many other dipoles.

00:25:27.880 --> 00:25:30.690 align:middle line:90%
So this is really--

00:25:30.690 --> 00:25:32.280 align:middle line:90%
this is basic physics.

00:25:32.280 --> 00:25:34.560 align:middle line:84%
You can see the first line,
forced equals mass times

00:25:34.560 --> 00:25:35.340 align:middle line:90%
acceleration.

00:25:35.340 --> 00:25:38.250 align:middle line:90%
Basic Newton's law.

00:25:38.250 --> 00:25:42.090 align:middle line:84%
And Newton also introduced
the calculus to us.

00:25:42.090 --> 00:25:47.490 align:middle line:84%
So he would be very comfortable
with the next line, which

00:25:47.490 --> 00:25:51.870 align:middle line:84%
is that force can be redefined
as the first derivative

00:25:51.870 --> 00:25:57.420 align:middle line:84%
of energy with respect
to position or radius.

00:25:57.420 --> 00:25:59.700 align:middle line:90%
And then mass is just mass.

00:25:59.700 --> 00:26:02.280 align:middle line:84%
And we're introducing the
subscript I, for the atomic.

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:06.780 align:middle line:84%
For each atom gets
its own Newton's law.

00:26:06.780 --> 00:26:11.070 align:middle line:84%
And then acceleration is just
a second derivative of position

00:26:11.070 --> 00:26:13.870 align:middle line:90%
with respect to time.

00:26:13.870 --> 00:26:16.390 align:middle line:84%
Now what kind of time constants
are we talking about here?

00:26:16.390 --> 00:26:19.530 align:middle line:84%
This is the femtosecond
range for atomic motion

00:26:19.530 --> 00:26:21.100 align:middle line:90%
to the minus 15 seconds.

00:26:21.100 --> 00:26:25.710 align:middle line:84%
And as you step through, you
update this kinetic procedure,

00:26:25.710 --> 00:26:27.990 align:middle line:84%
you can do it in
half time intervals,

00:26:27.990 --> 00:26:33.780 align:middle line:84%
updating velocity and
position every femtosecond

00:26:33.780 --> 00:26:35.940 align:middle line:90%
or half femtosecond.

00:26:35.940 --> 00:26:37.710 align:middle line:90%
So now what's this energy term?

00:26:37.710 --> 00:26:40.470 align:middle line:84%
This is what I alluded
to in the previous slide

00:26:40.470 --> 00:26:42.060 align:middle line:90%
as being very approximate.

00:26:42.060 --> 00:26:44.760 align:middle line:84%
And semi-empirical, it
is based on experiments

00:26:44.760 --> 00:26:46.620 align:middle line:84%
not entirely from
first principles

00:26:46.620 --> 00:26:49.170 align:middle line:84%
or not even from the
quantum approximations.

00:26:49.170 --> 00:26:52.620 align:middle line:84%
You have, say,
spectroscopic analyzes

00:26:52.620 --> 00:26:58.110 align:middle line:84%
that will show that the
spring-like motion that two

00:26:58.110 --> 00:27:00.810 align:middle line:84%
atoms can have when
they're connected by a bond

00:27:00.810 --> 00:27:05.500 align:middle line:84%
has a kind of a Hooke's
law type of spring motion.

00:27:05.500 --> 00:27:09.420 align:middle line:84%
And that's the energy
of the bond length,

00:27:09.420 --> 00:27:13.980 align:middle line:84%
EB in this sum of all
the E's, slide 52.

00:27:13.980 --> 00:27:19.680 align:middle line:84%
And E theta is the angle that
you have as a bond angle bends.

00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:21.840 align:middle line:84%
And that's the
spring-like force.

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:24.437 align:middle line:84%
And then omega is this
kind of torsion angle

00:27:24.437 --> 00:27:26.520 align:middle line:84%
that we've been talking
about in the phi PSI plot,

00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:28.800 align:middle line:90%
Ramachandran plot just before.

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:30.990 align:middle line:84%
Van der Waals is the
non-bonded contact,

00:27:30.990 --> 00:27:32.710 align:middle line:84%
which can be either
positive or negative.

00:27:32.710 --> 00:27:34.710 align:middle line:84%
Actually, it should show
down at the very bottom

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:39.480 align:middle line:84%
of the slide is that there
is a repulsive force, which

00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:44.130 align:middle line:84%
is related to the R
to the 12th power.

00:27:44.130 --> 00:27:47.530 align:middle line:84%
And an attractive force,
which is R to the sixth power.

00:27:47.530 --> 00:27:49.860 align:middle line:84%
So as you get closer, it
starts to get attracted

00:27:49.860 --> 00:27:53.010 align:middle line:84%
until you get this hard
sphere repulsion as you

00:27:53.010 --> 00:27:54.150 align:middle line:90%
get a little bit closer.

00:27:54.150 --> 00:27:58.230 align:middle line:84%
Electrostatic interactions
are the longest range effects.

00:27:58.230 --> 00:28:03.030 align:middle line:84%
All these covalent bonds,
B and theta and omega,

00:28:03.030 --> 00:28:03.910 align:middle line:90%
are short range.

00:28:03.910 --> 00:28:05.160 align:middle line:90%
Van der Waals are short range.

00:28:05.160 --> 00:28:09.180 align:middle line:84%
Electrostatic is slightly longer
range because it's a 1 over R,

00:28:09.180 --> 00:28:12.190 align:middle line:84%
or R is the distance
between the two atoms.

00:28:12.190 --> 00:28:13.920 align:middle line:84%
And those are the
main terms that

00:28:13.920 --> 00:28:15.510 align:middle line:84%
enter into all
molecular mechanics,

00:28:15.510 --> 00:28:17.135 align:middle line:84%
whether they're used
in crystallography

00:28:17.135 --> 00:28:18.900 align:middle line:84%
or whether they
used in abinitio.

00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:21.480 align:middle line:84%
Now this is the state
of the art for abinitio.

00:28:21.480 --> 00:28:26.070 align:middle line:84%
Just the very most
recent CAS competition

00:28:26.070 --> 00:28:30.660 align:middle line:84%
resulted in a very clear winner
by some criteria, at least.

00:28:30.660 --> 00:28:34.500 align:middle line:84%
The Baker Lab here,
the URL is down here,

00:28:34.500 --> 00:28:36.150 align:middle line:84%
is the number of
standard deviations

00:28:36.150 --> 00:28:40.650 align:middle line:84%
away for the mean in terms
of the score for the number

00:28:40.650 --> 00:28:43.290 align:middle line:84%
of correct predictions,
here out at around 30

00:28:43.290 --> 00:28:46.830 align:middle line:90%
where the mean is close to zero.

00:28:46.830 --> 00:28:53.400 align:middle line:84%
And even with this huge advance
for the field in prediction,

00:28:53.400 --> 00:28:57.690 align:middle line:84%
still this is a typical RMS
standard deviation between

00:28:57.690 --> 00:29:00.270 align:middle line:84%
the real structure, which was
kept hidden from sight from all

00:29:00.270 --> 00:29:02.610 align:middle line:84%
these competitors--
until it was known,

00:29:02.610 --> 00:29:05.320 align:middle line:90%
but not to the competitors--

00:29:05.320 --> 00:29:06.180 align:middle line:90%
and then revealed.

00:29:06.180 --> 00:29:09.750 align:middle line:84%
And the RMS deviation
was 6, or 4, or 5

00:29:09.750 --> 00:29:13.200 align:middle line:84%
in that range depending
on the structure

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:17.310 align:middle line:84%
and whether you include all the
atoms or just the core ones.

00:29:17.310 --> 00:29:20.070 align:middle line:84%
And this is not adequate,
as we saw in that slide

00:29:20.070 --> 00:29:23.310 align:middle line:84%
actually from the
same group earlier on.

00:29:23.310 --> 00:29:26.130 align:middle line:90%


00:29:26.130 --> 00:29:28.230 align:middle line:84%
Another way of looking
at this is now--

00:29:28.230 --> 00:29:31.620 align:middle line:84%
those were predictive
structures-- these are now

00:29:31.620 --> 00:29:32.760 align:middle line:90%
observed structures.

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:37.110 align:middle line:84%
The purple is comparing two
structures, both of which

00:29:37.110 --> 00:29:39.300 align:middle line:84%
were done by X-ray
crystallography.

00:29:39.300 --> 00:29:44.790 align:middle line:84%
And along the red axis here
is sequence identity, ranging

00:29:44.790 --> 00:29:48.370 align:middle line:84%
from 0 to close to 100%,
say 96 plus percent.

00:29:48.370 --> 00:29:51.960 align:middle line:84%
And the green axis
is the RMS root

00:29:51.960 --> 00:29:55.440 align:middle line:84%
mean square mediation
between structure one

00:29:55.440 --> 00:29:57.420 align:middle line:90%
and structure two.

00:29:57.420 --> 00:30:01.560 align:middle line:84%
And you can see that--
think of this purple curve

00:30:01.560 --> 00:30:04.650 align:middle line:84%
as starting in the lower
right, where you have

00:30:04.650 --> 00:30:10.440 align:middle line:84%
very high sequence identity and
less than 1 angstrom written

00:30:10.440 --> 00:30:12.420 align:middle line:90%
in deviation.

00:30:12.420 --> 00:30:16.620 align:middle line:90%


00:30:16.620 --> 00:30:19.357 align:middle line:84%
That means that when you
solve two proteins that

00:30:19.357 --> 00:30:20.940 align:middle line:84%
are very similar in
sequence, you will

00:30:20.940 --> 00:30:22.530 align:middle line:90%
get very similar structures.

00:30:22.530 --> 00:30:23.160 align:middle line:90%
That's good.

00:30:23.160 --> 00:30:25.170 align:middle line:84%
That bodes well for
structural modeling,

00:30:25.170 --> 00:30:26.880 align:middle line:84%
although that is not
structural modeling,

00:30:26.880 --> 00:30:28.740 align:middle line:90%
I mean, not homology modeling.

00:30:28.740 --> 00:30:31.320 align:middle line:84%
Then as you go down
in sequence identity,

00:30:31.320 --> 00:30:33.600 align:middle line:84%
the purple curve starts
to slope up and up

00:30:33.600 --> 00:30:39.060 align:middle line:84%
until it starts curving up
towards 2.74 and beyond.

00:30:39.060 --> 00:30:43.560 align:middle line:84%
it gets harder and harder to
do these structural alignments.

00:30:43.560 --> 00:30:48.945 align:middle line:84%
And so 4 angstroms
is the sort you

00:30:48.945 --> 00:30:51.630 align:middle line:84%
would get from homology
modeling at less than 20%

00:30:51.630 --> 00:30:53.740 align:middle line:90%
or 30% sequence identity.

00:30:53.740 --> 00:30:55.380 align:middle line:84%
And this is what I
said earlier, why

00:30:55.380 --> 00:30:58.080 align:middle line:84%
we're trying to get enough
proteins populating.

00:30:58.080 --> 00:31:00.510 align:middle line:84%
This is all known proteins
here being compared,

00:31:00.510 --> 00:31:02.550 align:middle line:90%
when enough populating it.

00:31:02.550 --> 00:31:08.820 align:middle line:84%
So you never have to go below
35% into this Twilight Zone,

00:31:08.820 --> 00:31:10.830 align:middle line:84%
where you really
can't make good--

00:31:10.830 --> 00:31:14.190 align:middle line:90%


00:31:14.190 --> 00:31:19.290 align:middle line:84%
you don't find good RMS
deviations between two

00:31:19.290 --> 00:31:23.280 align:middle line:90%
known crystal structures.

00:31:23.280 --> 00:31:28.980 align:middle line:84%
Now as we do protein dynamics
using the molecular mechanics

00:31:28.980 --> 00:31:31.620 align:middle line:84%
approximation we
talked about, these

00:31:31.620 --> 00:31:36.270 align:middle line:84%
can be applied not
only to predict

00:31:36.270 --> 00:31:42.810 align:middle line:84%
a static structure or a series
of steps in a protein process,

00:31:42.810 --> 00:31:46.590 align:middle line:84%
but the dynamics of folding from
a completely unfolded protein

00:31:46.590 --> 00:31:49.110 align:middle line:84%
as it might be coming
off the ribosome.

00:31:49.110 --> 00:31:51.840 align:middle line:84%
And this is something for
which there are relatively

00:31:51.840 --> 00:31:53.070 align:middle line:90%
few experimental methods.

00:31:53.070 --> 00:31:55.680 align:middle line:84%
And so this is clearly
a valuable contribution,

00:31:55.680 --> 00:31:59.640 align:middle line:84%
but there's a problem with
doing a theoretical calculation

00:31:59.640 --> 00:32:03.030 align:middle line:84%
that's hard to
empirically verify.

00:32:03.030 --> 00:32:07.110 align:middle line:84%
But in any case, to do
one of the larger tasks

00:32:07.110 --> 00:32:13.650 align:middle line:84%
and IBM and others are
sinking significant resources

00:32:13.650 --> 00:32:15.600 align:middle line:90%
and infrastructure of this.

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:20.130 align:middle line:84%
But doing your
femtosecond time scale

00:32:20.130 --> 00:32:23.610 align:middle line:84%
over a one microsecond
simulation, you can easily

00:32:23.610 --> 00:32:26.310 align:middle line:84%
do the math, that's
10 to the minus 6,

00:32:26.310 --> 00:32:29.190 align:middle line:84%
divided by 10 to
the -15 is about 10

00:32:29.190 --> 00:32:31.050 align:middle line:84%
to the ninth such
steps, each of which

00:32:31.050 --> 00:32:34.950 align:middle line:84%
involves this big calculation
that we just went through all

00:32:34.950 --> 00:32:36.750 align:middle line:90%
the energy terms log.

00:32:36.750 --> 00:32:38.230 align:middle line:90%
But that's been done for this.

00:32:38.230 --> 00:32:40.500 align:middle line:84%
And you can see the
blue and the red

00:32:40.500 --> 00:32:44.340 align:middle line:84%
represent the calculated
and the observed structure

00:32:44.340 --> 00:32:46.860 align:middle line:84%
at one point in the
dynamic simulation.

00:32:46.860 --> 00:32:49.470 align:middle line:90%


00:32:49.470 --> 00:32:52.020 align:middle line:84%
When you have a protein
three dimensional structure,

00:32:52.020 --> 00:32:54.960 align:middle line:84%
you can try to dock it
with small molecules.

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:57.620 align:middle line:84%
This could be
easier, in principle,

00:32:57.620 --> 00:33:00.120 align:middle line:84%
because you can keep both the
small molecule and the protein

00:33:00.120 --> 00:33:02.290 align:middle line:84%
relatively rigid
as you dock them.

00:33:02.290 --> 00:33:05.100 align:middle line:84%
There has to be some flexibility
hence the name, flex,

00:33:05.100 --> 00:33:07.560 align:middle line:90%
for one of these programs.

00:33:07.560 --> 00:33:11.700 align:middle line:84%
And overall, the results
are intriguing enough

00:33:11.700 --> 00:33:15.060 align:middle line:84%
that you might want to use it as
an alternative in the few cases

00:33:15.060 --> 00:33:16.560 align:middle line:84%
where you have the
three dimensional

00:33:16.560 --> 00:33:18.450 align:middle line:84%
structure of a protein,
but for some reason

00:33:18.450 --> 00:33:20.010 align:middle line:84%
you can't solve the
three dimensional

00:33:20.010 --> 00:33:22.360 align:middle line:90%
structure of the complex.

00:33:22.360 --> 00:33:24.100 align:middle line:84%
But you must remember
that actually,

00:33:24.100 --> 00:33:27.210 align:middle line:84%
even though we cited that the
solving of protein structure

00:33:27.210 --> 00:33:30.757 align:middle line:84%
might be $100,000,
solving a complex once you

00:33:30.757 --> 00:33:32.340 align:middle line:84%
have the protein
structure is actually

00:33:32.340 --> 00:33:33.507 align:middle line:90%
considerably less than that.

00:33:33.507 --> 00:33:36.480 align:middle line:84%
But in any case,
this is encouraging

00:33:36.480 --> 00:33:41.700 align:middle line:84%
where you have in the order
of 0.25 to 1.84 as a root mean

00:33:41.700 --> 00:33:44.520 align:middle line:84%
squared deviation
between the predicted

00:33:44.520 --> 00:33:47.070 align:middle line:84%
and the experimental binding
modes of small molecule.

00:33:47.070 --> 00:33:51.300 align:middle line:84%
You can imagine that to
be off by 1.8 angstroms,

00:33:51.300 --> 00:33:53.760 align:middle line:84%
it must be docking in
roughly the right pocket,

00:33:53.760 --> 00:33:58.290 align:middle line:84%
but maybe at the wrong angle
or yeah, maybe slightly off.

00:33:58.290 --> 00:34:01.820 align:middle line:90%


00:34:01.820 --> 00:34:06.560 align:middle line:84%
So the last topic is
the issue of cross talk.

00:34:06.560 --> 00:34:09.170 align:middle line:84%
As we talk about protein
three dimensional structures,

00:34:09.170 --> 00:34:10.940 align:middle line:90%
we try to find homologs.

00:34:10.940 --> 00:34:14.900 align:middle line:84%
And we often find homologs
within an organism, pair logs.

00:34:14.900 --> 00:34:19.100 align:middle line:84%
And these pair logs and
alternative splice forms

00:34:19.100 --> 00:34:22.340 align:middle line:84%
of a protein are
potential toxic side

00:34:22.340 --> 00:34:24.800 align:middle line:90%
reactions of a particular drug.

00:34:24.800 --> 00:34:26.630 align:middle line:84%
And you can see that
many of these drugs

00:34:26.630 --> 00:34:28.880 align:middle line:90%
are aimed at family members.

00:34:28.880 --> 00:34:33.320 align:middle line:84%
For example, a top two are
part of the steroid binding

00:34:33.320 --> 00:34:38.389 align:middle line:84%
family, which we have
already introduced once

00:34:38.389 --> 00:34:41.909 align:middle line:84%
and it will be in
an upcoming slide.

00:34:41.909 --> 00:34:51.338 align:middle line:84%
And when you consider that these
proteins, that particular class

00:34:51.338 --> 00:34:53.630 align:middle line:84%
of proteins interacts both
with a small molecule, which

00:34:53.630 --> 00:34:58.280 align:middle line:84%
is either a natural or
artificial steroid or thyroid

00:34:58.280 --> 00:35:04.650 align:middle line:84%
like which is a
steroid like compound,

00:35:04.650 --> 00:35:07.540 align:middle line:84%
and it binds to a
target nucleic acid.

00:35:07.540 --> 00:35:10.470 align:middle line:84%
And both the nucleic acid
and the small molecule

00:35:10.470 --> 00:35:12.480 align:middle line:90%
have potential for crosstalk.

00:35:12.480 --> 00:35:15.580 align:middle line:84%
And here is the nucleic
acid part of the story.

00:35:15.580 --> 00:35:17.670 align:middle line:84%
And in the next slide will
show the small molecule

00:35:17.670 --> 00:35:18.690 align:middle line:90%
part of the story.

00:35:18.690 --> 00:35:22.530 align:middle line:84%
But the nucleic acid part,
you have two protein domains

00:35:22.530 --> 00:35:23.760 align:middle line:90%
similar to one another.

00:35:23.760 --> 00:35:25.830 align:middle line:84%
This is another
example of the symmetry

00:35:25.830 --> 00:35:28.560 align:middle line:84%
that we started this talk
and ended last talk with.

00:35:28.560 --> 00:35:32.340 align:middle line:84%
The symmetry here is,
you have these two that

00:35:32.340 --> 00:35:35.790 align:middle line:84%
can be direct or
inverted repeats,

00:35:35.790 --> 00:35:38.410 align:middle line:84%
separated by little
spacers here.

00:35:38.410 --> 00:35:40.980 align:middle line:84%
So the DNA is in yellow
and the little spacers

00:35:40.980 --> 00:35:45.060 align:middle line:90%
are in the gray and CPK colors.

00:35:45.060 --> 00:35:47.700 align:middle line:84%
And the protein domains
are in green and white,

00:35:47.700 --> 00:35:49.620 align:middle line:84%
where the green and
white are structurally

00:35:49.620 --> 00:35:50.920 align:middle line:90%
similar to one another.

00:35:50.920 --> 00:35:54.510 align:middle line:84%
It's hard for you appreciate
them going around like that.

00:35:54.510 --> 00:36:01.980 align:middle line:84%
This is to emphasize that the
direct or inverted repeat here.

00:36:01.980 --> 00:36:04.380 align:middle line:90%
Now that's the DNA interaction.

00:36:04.380 --> 00:36:07.320 align:middle line:90%
And this is the ligand binding.

00:36:07.320 --> 00:36:12.870 align:middle line:84%
You can see the estradiol
is the small, yellow ligand.

00:36:12.870 --> 00:36:17.500 align:middle line:84%
And the tamoxifen, which
is the larger ligand.

00:36:17.500 --> 00:36:23.480 align:middle line:84%
This is something that's
important in treating

00:36:23.480 --> 00:36:28.250 align:middle line:84%
breast cancer that might
be responsive to estrogen

00:36:28.250 --> 00:36:32.000 align:middle line:90%
binding drugs.

00:36:32.000 --> 00:36:34.870 align:middle line:84%
So this is the part of
the protein that has

00:36:34.870 --> 00:36:37.200 align:middle line:90%
two parts, or three parts here.

00:36:37.200 --> 00:36:38.680 align:middle line:90%
That's the binding domain.

00:36:38.680 --> 00:36:40.780 align:middle line:84%
The little red thing
is an activator peptide

00:36:40.780 --> 00:36:44.200 align:middle line:84%
and then there's the
DNA binding component.

00:36:44.200 --> 00:36:46.690 align:middle line:84%
Now what are the
crosstalk we have here?

00:36:46.690 --> 00:36:52.810 align:middle line:84%
You can see that
these wide variety

00:36:52.810 --> 00:36:58.570 align:middle line:84%
of different steroid-like
protein binding domains,

00:36:58.570 --> 00:37:04.590 align:middle line:84%
they bind a vitamin
D3, retinoic acids

00:37:04.590 --> 00:37:10.110 align:middle line:84%
such as those that occur
in developmental processes

00:37:10.110 --> 00:37:11.430 align:middle line:90%
and in vision.

00:37:11.430 --> 00:37:13.830 align:middle line:84%
Thyroid, which regulates
our metabolism.

00:37:13.830 --> 00:37:17.760 align:middle line:84%
And estrogen,
testosterone, and so forth.

00:37:17.760 --> 00:37:20.730 align:middle line:84%
All of these things have
fairly similar small molecule

00:37:20.730 --> 00:37:21.720 align:middle line:90%
binding sites.

00:37:21.720 --> 00:37:24.840 align:middle line:84%
And the DNA sequences they bind
are these half sites, which

00:37:24.840 --> 00:37:26.910 align:middle line:84%
are very closely
conserved in all

00:37:26.910 --> 00:37:28.870 align:middle line:90%
the members of this big family.

00:37:28.870 --> 00:37:31.710 align:middle line:84%
And one of the main
differences is the distance

00:37:31.710 --> 00:37:33.390 align:middle line:90%
between these can vary.

00:37:33.390 --> 00:37:35.190 align:middle line:84%
And the distance
here is indicated

00:37:35.190 --> 00:37:38.850 align:middle line:84%
on the far left hand
lower left here. dR3,

00:37:38.850 --> 00:37:41.280 align:middle line:84%
means direct repeat
with three nucleotides

00:37:41.280 --> 00:37:44.400 align:middle line:84%
in between those
two halves sides.

00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:48.000 align:middle line:84%
IR0 means an inverted
repeat with 0 nucleotides

00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:49.410 align:middle line:90%
between the half sites.

00:37:49.410 --> 00:37:52.560 align:middle line:84%
The R15 is direct repeat with
15 nucleotides, and so on.

00:37:52.560 --> 00:37:55.020 align:middle line:84%
And you see each
family member has

00:37:55.020 --> 00:37:57.252 align:middle line:84%
a distinct ligand
and nucleic acid.

00:37:57.252 --> 00:37:59.460 align:middle line:84%
Although, there's a lot of
similarity of the ligands,

00:37:59.460 --> 00:38:01.980 align:middle line:84%
a lot of similarity
of the nucleic acids.

00:38:01.980 --> 00:38:04.890 align:middle line:84%
How do we-- last line
of this slide 61--

00:38:04.890 --> 00:38:08.380 align:middle line:84%
target one member of this
protein family or other protein

00:38:08.380 --> 00:38:08.880 align:middle line:90%
families?

00:38:08.880 --> 00:38:12.150 align:middle line:90%


00:38:12.150 --> 00:38:15.660 align:middle line:84%
In some cases, you will have
complete artistic control,

00:38:15.660 --> 00:38:19.920 align:middle line:84%
not only on the small molecule,
but of the protein itself.

00:38:19.920 --> 00:38:23.220 align:middle line:84%
If you have a small molecule
that looks like ATP,

00:38:23.220 --> 00:38:28.500 align:middle line:84%
you can inhibit all sorts
of ATP binding proteins.

00:38:28.500 --> 00:38:31.510 align:middle line:84%
If you're lucky, you can inhibit
a specific class of ATP binding

00:38:31.510 --> 00:38:32.010 align:middle line:90%
proteins.

00:38:32.010 --> 00:38:35.520 align:middle line:84%
But knocking out a particular
member of a class is hard.

00:38:35.520 --> 00:38:38.730 align:middle line:84%
And you can see here on the
right hand side, these three

00:38:38.730 --> 00:38:39.930 align:middle line:90%
chemical structures.

00:38:39.930 --> 00:38:45.960 align:middle line:84%
The adenine part are these five
and six member fused rings.

00:38:45.960 --> 00:38:49.420 align:middle line:84%
And attach to them
are the side chains.

00:38:49.420 --> 00:38:51.660 align:middle line:84%
The first one, all
black structure,

00:38:51.660 --> 00:38:56.010 align:middle line:84%
is a known inhibitor of
protein kinases in general.

00:38:56.010 --> 00:39:00.030 align:middle line:84%
And the red additions are how to
make that a little bit bulkier

00:39:00.030 --> 00:39:04.680 align:middle line:84%
so that it will no longer bind
to protein kinases in general.

00:39:04.680 --> 00:39:07.875 align:middle line:84%
Now why would you want to
make this inhibitor not bind

00:39:07.875 --> 00:39:08.670 align:middle line:90%
to protein kinases?

00:39:08.670 --> 00:39:11.130 align:middle line:84%
Well now, if it doesn't
bind any protein kinase

00:39:11.130 --> 00:39:13.650 align:middle line:84%
very well because it's too
bulky, it doesn't fit anymore,

00:39:13.650 --> 00:39:16.770 align:middle line:84%
then you can carve
an amino acid out

00:39:16.770 --> 00:39:25.380 align:middle line:84%
of one of the protein kinases by
doing homologous recombination

00:39:25.380 --> 00:39:29.700 align:middle line:84%
or transgenic mutating that
particular-- the nucleic acid

00:39:29.700 --> 00:39:31.110 align:middle line:90%
encoding that gene.

00:39:31.110 --> 00:39:33.780 align:middle line:84%
And you will have this
ability to manipulate

00:39:33.780 --> 00:39:39.027 align:middle line:84%
both the chemical and the
protein target in cases where--

00:39:39.027 --> 00:39:40.860 align:middle line:84%
as we'll get to in the
last three lectures--

00:39:40.860 --> 00:39:43.830 align:middle line:84%
where we're analyzing
systems Biology networks,

00:39:43.830 --> 00:39:47.550 align:middle line:84%
you want to be able to target
a particular protein at a time

00:39:47.550 --> 00:39:51.930 align:middle line:84%
by having a known ligand protein
interaction where you minimize

00:39:51.930 --> 00:39:54.990 align:middle line:84%
the cross plot by engineering
the specific interaction.

00:39:54.990 --> 00:39:58.110 align:middle line:84%
You start with a specific
interaction for the class

00:39:58.110 --> 00:40:00.570 align:middle line:84%
and then you engineer it
so it hits one of them.

00:40:00.570 --> 00:40:02.280 align:middle line:84%
So that way you can
do a time course,

00:40:02.280 --> 00:40:05.100 align:middle line:84%
say, just knocking out
that particular protein

00:40:05.100 --> 00:40:07.710 align:middle line:90%
quickly or letting it come back.

00:40:07.710 --> 00:40:10.972 align:middle line:84%
And this shows the results
down at the bottom here.

00:40:10.972 --> 00:40:12.930 align:middle line:84%
You start out with these
two different kinases.

00:40:12.930 --> 00:40:16.620 align:middle line:90%


00:40:16.620 --> 00:40:20.340 align:middle line:90%
CDK2, involved in cell cycling.

00:40:20.340 --> 00:40:25.590 align:middle line:90%
And chem kinase.

00:40:25.590 --> 00:40:31.320 align:middle line:84%
Two, both of these would bind
to the original black inhibitor.

00:40:31.320 --> 00:40:35.100 align:middle line:84%
And hence, there would
be significant crosstalk.

00:40:35.100 --> 00:40:40.530 align:middle line:84%
And here, the interfering
dosages in micromolars

00:40:40.530 --> 00:40:42.450 align:middle line:84%
are shown in the
three columns here

00:40:42.450 --> 00:40:43.980 align:middle line:84%
for the three
different compounds,

00:40:43.980 --> 00:40:45.400 align:middle line:90%
run underneath each of them.

00:40:45.400 --> 00:40:53.820 align:middle line:84%
And you can see that
the lower the number,

00:40:53.820 --> 00:40:54.920 align:middle line:90%
the better it binds.

00:40:54.920 --> 00:40:57.080 align:middle line:90%
So when you take these--

00:40:57.080 --> 00:41:02.540 align:middle line:84%
if you take these now binding
pockets and carve them out,

00:41:02.540 --> 00:41:04.040 align:middle line:84%
that's what the
little wedge cut out

00:41:04.040 --> 00:41:10.670 align:middle line:84%
for the two lowest ones,
CDK2, derivative AS1, and chem

00:41:10.670 --> 00:41:14.030 align:middle line:90%
kinase 2, AS1 derivative.

00:41:14.030 --> 00:41:16.130 align:middle line:84%
Now that you can see,
they have a much improved

00:41:16.130 --> 00:41:20.870 align:middle line:84%
binding constant to even
the bulkiest derivatives.

00:41:20.870 --> 00:41:26.360 align:middle line:84%
And this is mediated by a
threonine or phenylalanine

00:41:26.360 --> 00:41:28.730 align:middle line:84%
at position 38 is
changed to a glycine.

00:41:28.730 --> 00:41:30.860 align:middle line:84%
Glycine is obviously
smaller than a threonine

00:41:30.860 --> 00:41:32.720 align:middle line:84%
or phenylalanine
at that position

00:41:32.720 --> 00:41:37.910 align:middle line:84%
and makes room for the
drug just in the same way

00:41:37.910 --> 00:41:40.250 align:middle line:84%
that changing the tyrosine
to a phenylalanine

00:41:40.250 --> 00:41:42.440 align:middle line:84%
made room for the
dideoxy terminator

00:41:42.440 --> 00:41:44.790 align:middle line:90%
in the earlier example.

00:41:44.790 --> 00:41:49.160 align:middle line:84%
So in summary, we have talked
about protein three dimensional

00:41:49.160 --> 00:41:52.640 align:middle line:84%
structure and how we can
program proteins, basically.

00:41:52.640 --> 00:41:56.120 align:middle line:84%
How we can use bits of
proteins that we may not

00:41:56.120 --> 00:41:57.980 align:middle line:90%
be able to predict, a priori--

00:41:57.980 --> 00:42:00.920 align:middle line:84%
from scratch-- how we get
from a sequence to a ligand.

00:42:00.920 --> 00:42:03.110 align:middle line:84%
But we can take
parts that we know

00:42:03.110 --> 00:42:05.240 align:middle line:84%
and rearrange them in
interesting combinations.

00:42:05.240 --> 00:42:07.910 align:middle line:84%
We can build up databases
of binding constants

00:42:07.910 --> 00:42:11.570 align:middle line:84%
to combinations of combinatorial
libraries of nucleic acids,

00:42:11.570 --> 00:42:13.820 align:middle line:90%
of peptides, of small molecules.

00:42:13.820 --> 00:42:17.240 align:middle line:84%
And we can put these together
in novel combinations that

00:42:17.240 --> 00:42:20.450 align:middle line:84%
allow us to do network
analysis and ask

00:42:20.450 --> 00:42:26.180 align:middle line:90%
what protein does what event.

00:42:26.180 --> 00:42:27.770 align:middle line:90%
So thank you.

00:42:27.770 --> 00:42:29.650 align:middle line:90%
Until next time.

00:42:29.650 --> 00:42:32.000 align:middle line:90%